{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Adonai, why have You harmed</b> — It is written with אלף-דלת (i.e., as אדני), for the particular Name, which is the attribute of mercy, would not bring harm upon the people. And above, Moses mentioned it twice with אלף-דלת (Exodus 4:10, 13), for he was pleading with it that anger not burn in Him. And perhaps he was afraid to mention the great Name [YHWH], newly revealed to him and spoken to him." "<b>Why have You harmed</b> — After Elohim had informed Moses twice (above 3:19, 4:21) that the king of Egypt would not let them go, why did he complain?\n\nAnd R. Abraham ibn Ezra (on Exodus 5:22) said that Moses thought that from the time he spoke to Pharaoh by the mission of HaShem, the burden upon them would lighten and Elohim would begin to deliver them — but behold, Pharaoh made it heavier and worse. And this is the meaning of <b>why have You harmed</b>, for this is the opposite of what You said to me: 'I have surely seen the affliction of My people, and I have come down to deliver them' (Exodus 3:7–8). But this is not correct in my eyes, because he said <b>and You have not delivered</b>, and there is no 'deliverance' other than their departure from exile.\n\nAnd in my opinion, Moses our master thought that HaShem would say that Pharaoh would not heed them to release them by his command, and not through sign and wonder, until He would perform His many wonders upon him — but he thought that He would bring them upon him one immediately after another within a few days. And when he [Pharaoh] said 'I do not know YHWH,' he [Moses] would be commanded to perform immediately before him the sign of the serpent, and he [Pharaoh] would not heed, and he would strike him with it that very day, with blood, and afterward with all the plagues. And when he saw that three days had passed and he [Pharaoh] was harming them every day, and HaShem did not strike him, and did not reveal Himself to Moses to inform him what to do — then Moses thought that it would be prolonged.\n\nAnd it is possible that in this event which Scripture relates there were many days, for when the officers of the children of Israel were beaten, they stood [waiting] days until they spoke with Pharaoh himself, and they said to him, <b>'Why do you do thus to your servants?'</b> (Exodus 5:15) — for it is not within the authority of any person to enter the inner palace of a king and speak to him face to face, even the officers of the people, who were contemptible in his eyes. And behold, they endured their toil and their oppression for many days and would come even to the gate of the king, until their outcry was heard before the king, and he commanded that they come before him and speak with him. And likewise it is possible that Moses returned to HaShem and said <b>why have You harmed</b> after days had passed from the time the officers encountered them.\n\nAnd so in Shemot Rabbah (5:19) they said: 'And the taskmasters of the people and their officers went out' (Exodus 5:10) — once he [Pharaoh] decreed thus, Moses went to Midian and stayed there six months, and Aaron was sitting in Egypt, and at that time Moses sent his wife and sons back to Midian. And they further said (ibid. 5:24): 'And they met Moses and Aaron' (Exodus 5:20) — after six months the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to Moses in Midian and said to him, 'Go, return to Egypt.' Moses came from Midian and Aaron from Egypt, and the officers of Israel met them as they were coming out from before Pharaoh.\n\nAnd likewise I saw further in Midrash Chazita (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 2:22): '\"My beloved is like a gazelle\" (Song of Songs 2:9) — just as this gazelle appears and again appears, so the first redeemer appeared to them, and again was hidden from them, and again appeared to them. And how long was he hidden from them? Rabbi Tanhuma said: three months. That is what is written: \"And they met Moses and Aaron.\" And Rabbi Yehudah bar Rabbi says: for intervals (לקצין). That is to say, the language of פגיעה (meeting/encounter) denotes [occurrences] at intervals.'\n\nAnd behold, many days had passed from the time HaShem spoke to him and he came to Pharaoh. Therefore, when Moses returned to HaShem, he said: <b>Why have You harmed the people</b> — why send me hastily before the appointed end had arrived? For it was not fitting to send me until You were willing to deliver them — for now You have harmed them and not delivered them, and if so You act toward them, they will continue to suffer through the evil that befalls them.\n\nAnd therefore He answered him: <b>Now</b> — at this time, soon — <b>you shall see what I will do to Pharaoh</b>, for I will not prolong it as much as you thought, and its time is near to come and its days will not be drawn out."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {1 "The structure follows Ramban's typical pattern: citation of Ibn Ezra's view, refutation, and his own interpretation. The abbreviation ר\"א is resolved as Rabbi Abraham (ibn Ezra). לקצין is translated hyper-literally as 'for intervals'; the word is rare and its precise sense is debated."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:31.652208826Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}